/* Baker School of Aeronautics (2022) - Powerplant oral question banks. */
window.BAKER_POWERPLANT_BANKS=[{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-a","letter":"A","title":"A. Reciprocating Engines","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-A-001","question":"How are conventional reciprocating engines classified?","idealAnswer":"cad They are classified according to cylinder arrangement with respect to the crankshaft (in-line, vtype, ww radial, and opposed), or according to method of cooling (liquid cooled, or air cooled).","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-002","question":"What are the different types of piston rings?","idealAnswer":"Compression rings, Oil control rings, and Scraper rings. \" o","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-003","question":"What are the main causes of a cylinder having low compression?","idealAnswer":"Broken piston rings, rings that are not seating properly, burnt valves, and poorly seating valves. 7 bel","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-004","question":"What is the purpose of the oil control rings?","idealAnswer":"They are used Lo control the thickness of the oil film on the cylinder * walls. x","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-005","question":"What may be the result of installing piston rings incorrectly?","idealAnswer":"Excessive oil consumption. bd ~","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-006","question":"What type of piston rods are commonly found in radial engines?","idealAnswer":"A master and articulating rod assembly.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-007","question":"What type of bearings are in general use in reciprocating engines?","idealAnswer":"Plain, roller, and ball bearings. Plain bearings are @ generally used for crankshaft, camshafts, and connecting rods. Roller bearings are used primarily as crankshaft main a bearings. And ball bearings are used for supercharger impeller shaft bearings and crankshaft thrust bearings on i large radial engines. cs >","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-008","question":"What is the indication of valve blow-by?","idealAnswer":"Valve blow-by is indicated by a hissing or whistle when pulling the = propeller through by hand. A cylinder compression check should be completed to identify the faulty cylinder.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-009","question":"What is the purpose of using more than one spring for valve closing?","idealAnswer":"n Valve springs will resonate (or surge) at certain engine speeds. If only one spring is used, the + valve will float as the spring surges. To eliminate this problem, two springs (one inside the other) are installed on each valve. Each spring will resonate at a different engine speed, y eliminating valve float. Lal .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-010","question":"What is the purpose of valve overlap?","idealAnswer":"Valve overlap permits better volumetric efficiency and lowers the cylinder = operating temperatures. ° re","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-011","question":"What is the purpose of using valves with sodium filled stems?","idealAnswer":"e Some intake and exhaust valve stems are hollow and partially filled with metallic sodium. Sodium is used because it is an excellent heat conductor. The sodium melts at a relatively low 6 temperature, and as the valve moves up and down the sodium is circulated, carrying the heat from e the valve head to the stem. Then the heat is dissipated to the cylinder head.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-012","question":"What causes engine sudden stoppage?","idealAnswer":"Striking an object or engine seizure due to internal damage. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-013","question":"What type of inspection must be made after sudden stoppage of an engine?","idealAnswer":"Most manufacturers require a complete engine tear down and inspection.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-014","question":"What is the purpose of the crankahall dynamic dampers used in aircraft engines?","idealAnswer":"Dampers are used to overcome forces which cause deflection of the crankshaft and torsional vibration, These forces are generated by the power impulses of the pistons. Crankshaft vibrations are reduced by placing floating dampers (weights) in the counterweight assembly, Particularly in a single throw type crankshaft,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-015","question":"How would you make a \"Hinout! check on a crankshaft that Is still installed in the engine?","idealAnswer":"Remove the propeller from the crankshaft and attach a dial indicator gauge to the front of the crankcase, Adjust the position of the gauge needle so that It is touching the shaft. Rotate the crankshaft and check for misalignment, Nefer to the table of limits to check the allowance. Flanged crankshafts must also be chacked from the front face of the flange.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-016","question":"What Is detonation?","idealAnswer":"4 Normal combustion Is when the fuel-alr mixture burns at a uniform rate across the combustion . chamber, The temperature and pressure within the cylinder rise at a normal rate as the . mixture burns, All fuels have critical limits of temperature and compression, beyond this limit they will Ignite spontaneously and burn with explosive violence. This instantaneous explosive burning of the last portion of the charge Is called detonation.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-017","question":"What cylinder Is engine Lo magneto timing based on?","idealAnswer":"The number one cylinder, Timing Is usually set for around 30 degrees before top dead center of the compression stroke,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-018","question":"What is engine sudden speed reduction?","idealAnswer":"This occurs when the propeller strikes an object but continues to run. When this occurs, a thorough external engine Inspection should be carried out, including an oil analysis. The propeller should also be Inspected along with a run-out check,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-019","question":"What can be learned about the condition of an engine by studying the results of a compression check?","idealAnswer":"From the cylinder compression check you can determine if the valves, piston rings, and pistons are adequately sealing the combustion chamber,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-020","question":"How can a cold cylinder be located on # double row radial engine?","idealAnswer":"Either an old tool called a magic wand or a more modern Infrared laser thermometer,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-021","question":"What Instrument can be used to check bore for out of roundness?","idealAnswer":"Acylinder bore can be checked with a dial Indicator, telescopic gauge, and outside micrometer, or inside micrometer,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-022","question":"What would a cold cylinder indicate?","idealAnswer":"That there has been no combustion inside the cylinder, normally due to no ignition or a clogged fuel nozzle,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-023","question":"Where Is a crack most likely to occur ona piston?","idealAnswer":"A crack is most likely to occur where the piston pin/wrist pin connects, The metal is thinnest at this location,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-024","question":"What should be done to a nose wheel airplane before removing the engine?","idealAnswer":"Support the tall of the airplane,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-A-025","question":"What ls the running valve Clearance on engines equipped with hydraulic lifters?","idealAnswer":"Zero . rs","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-b","letter":"B","title":"B. Turbine Engines","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-B-001","question":"What are the major components of a typical gas turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"A typical gas turbine engine consists of an air inlet, compression section, combustion section, turbine section, exhaust section, and accessory section. }","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-002","question":"What are the two principal types of compressors used in turbojet aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The two most common compressors in use today are the centrifugal flow and axial flow types.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-003","question":"What are the four combustion chamber systems?","idealAnswer":"They are the can, can-annular, annular, and reverse flow } annular types.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-004","question":"What is the purpose of the interconnector tubes between can type combustion chambers?","idealAnswer":"To spread the flame to the combustion chambers that are not equipped with igniter plugs during engine starting.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-005","question":"What prevents burning of the liner walls in can type combustion chambers?","idealAnswer":"Louvers are provided along the axial length of the liners to direct a cooling layer of air along the inside wall of the liner. 3 6. What types of damage may be found when inspecting compressor blades? F Dents, scratches, gouges, galling, burns, burrs, pitting, and cracks.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-006","question":"What is the location and function of the diffuser section in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"The diffuser is the divergent section of the engine located between the compressor section and burner cans. Its function is to change the high velocity compressor discharge air to static ; pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-007","question":"Where are stress rupture cracks most likely to occur on turbine blades?","idealAnswer":"They usually appear as small hairline cracks on or across the leading or trailing edge of the blades. The cracks appear at right angles to the edge length.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-008","question":"How are compressor blades attached to a disk-type rotor?","idealAnswer":"Fi The compressor blades are fitted into the disks by either bulb-type, fir-tree, or dove tail type , roots. The blades are then locked by means of screws, peening, locking wires, pins, or keys. } '","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-009","question":"What is the purpose of the nozzle diaphragm, or turbine nozzle?","idealAnswer":"t The turbine nozzle prepares the combustion mass airflow to drive the turbine rotor. First, they change a portion of the heat and pressure energy to velocity energy. The fixed vanes are shaped and set at such an angle that they form several small nozzles that discharge the gases at extremely high speeds. The second purpose of the turbine nozzle is to deflect the gases so they strike the turbine at the most efficient angle for maximum turbine rotation.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-010","question":"What is the major difference between turboprop and turbojet engines?","idealAnswer":"A turboprop engine usually has more turbine stages than the turbojet engine. In addition to operating the compressor and accessories, the turboprop turbine must also drive a propeller. a = SR sl","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-011","question":"What is a turbofan engine?","idealAnswer":"A turbofan engine is in principle, the same as a turboprop, except that the propeller is . replaced by a duct-enclosed axial flow fan.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-012","question":"What is the effect of air density on the thrust of a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":") Air density determines the mass of air that is used by a jet engine. The factors that affect density : are the temperature and pressure of the air. If density increases, thrust increases, and vice versa. An increase in air temperature entering an engine will cause a decrease in air density and thrust. An increase in air pressure entering the engine will cause an increase in air density and thrust. 1} With a given throttle setting, engine thrust will vary if the temperature or pressure of the air entering the engine changes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-013","question":"What are the two most common types of thrust reversers?","idealAnswer":"They are mechanical blockage type and aerodynamic 4 blockage type.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-014","question":"What is a split compressor system in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"a The split compressor system requires two concentric shafts joining the turbine stages to their respective 1 compressors.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-015","question":"What is one method of relieving thermal stress on a turbine disk?","idealAnswer":"One method is to direct cooling bleed air back ‘onto the face of the disk.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-016","question":"What may be the indications if a turbojet engine is out of trim?","idealAnswer":"The engine has high exhaust gas temperature (EGT at a target engine pressure ratio (EPR) for \\ takeoff.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-017","question":"What is a free turbine?","idealAnswer":"A turbine wheel that, rather than driving the compressor rotor, drives a propeller or helicopter transmission. \"","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-B-018","question":"What is the main purpose of thrust reversers?","idealAnswer":". For slowing the aircraft down to effective braking speeds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-c","letter":"C","title":"C. Engine Inspection","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-C-001","question":"What steps must be taken to prepare an aircraft for a 100-hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"Remove or open all necessary inspection plates, access doors, fairing and cowling. Then thoroughly clean then aircraft and the engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-002","question":"What may be used as a guide for a 100-hour inspection on aircraft engines?","idealAnswer":"FAR Part 43, Appendix D.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-003","question":"What additional inspection must be performed if the cylinder compression is weak?","idealAnswer":"You must make an internal cylinder inspection for improper internal tolerances.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-004","question":"Where are the aircraft engine operating limitations found?","idealAnswer":"In the Aircraft Specifications or Type Certificate Data Sheet.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-005","question":"Where can a mechanic look to identify an aircraft engine?","idealAnswer":"On the engine identification plate that is affixed to the engine in an accessible location.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-006","question":"What publication is needed to inspect an engine for conformity with specifications?","idealAnswer":". The Engine Specifications or Type Certificate Data Sheet.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-007","question":"What type of FAA approval is required when a change to an engine design is not enough to require a new Type certificate?","idealAnswer":"A Supplemental Type Certificate is required. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-008","question":"What type of FAA approval is required when a change is made to the engine principle of operation?","idealAnswer":"Anew Type Certificate must be obtained.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-009","question":"What instrument used for internal inspections has been a mainstay of engine inspections for many years?","idealAnswer":"The Borescope is a precision optical instrument used for remote visual inspections of internal inaccessible areas.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-010","question":"What additional inspection should you perform if you find a cylinder has weak compression?","idealAnswer":"Cylinder leak-down test.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-C-011","question":"How does an oil analysis program help to evaluate the internal condition of an engine?","idealAnswer":"oil analysis is system that establishes a trend to measure engine health. One test does not do much unless something is very wrong. ‘ ® = = 2) ve E) be) had e 3Section V - Powerplant Systems and * Components = : Oral Questions Section V - Powerplant Systems and Components (12 Subjects) Lcngine Instrument Systems _ Subject H 78 cngine Fire Protection cngine Electrical Systems Subject J 81 | ® brication Systems 7 Subject K 83 ignition and Starting Systems Subject L Wel Metering Systems Subject M ®ngine Fuel Systems | Subject N 90 Bengine Cooling Systems Subject P 93 ®ngine Exhaust and Reverser Systems Subject Q 95 Propellers Subject R |97 Mrurbine Powered Auxiliary Power Units --SSSSS=«dSubject'S «99 [od rd td a a rd Pe @ fad \" 77 a So LROR AE CPR en = PE a ae","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-h","letter":"H","title":"H. Engine Instrument Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-H-001","question":"What are the units in an engine fuel flow system?","idealAnswer":"The system consists of a transmitter and an indicator for each @e engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-002","question":"Where is the fuel flow transmitter located?","idealAnswer":"It is mounted in the fuel line between the engine driven pump and the ¢€ carburetor. ¢","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-003","question":"What type of readout is indicated on a fuel flow system?","idealAnswer":"The indicator is calibrated to record the fuel flow in € pounds per hour. ¢€","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-004","question":"What exactly does a mass fuel flow transmitter measure?","idealAnswer":"The density mass of the fuel that flows through it. ¢","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-005","question":"What is the reason for monitoring the fuel flow?","idealAnswer":"« In addition to fuel consumption, the operator can determine from the fuel flow indication whether the ¢ engine is operating at the correct fuel-air mixture for a given power setting. e¢","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-006","question":"What docs a manifold pressure gauge indicate?","idealAnswer":"It measures absolute pressure in the intake manifold.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-007","question":"What would be the effect of a broken manifold pressure gauge line?","idealAnswer":"e A broken line will cause the gauge to indicate atmospheric pressure. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-008","question":"What does the engine tachometer indicate?","idealAnswer":"It indicates crankshaft speed (RPM). e e-","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-009","question":"What is the most likely cause of an oscillating tachometer?","idealAnswer":"A kink in the cable. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-010","question":"What type of tachometer is used in multi-engine airplanes?","idealAnswer":"Alternating current tachometer generators. - e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-011","question":"What are turbine engine tachometers designed to indicate?","idealAnswer":"They are designed to indicated percent of engine a rotor (shaft) RPM. al","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-012","question":"What is the EPR on a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"It is a ratio between total turbine discharge pressure to total inlet pressure o and is an indication of the thrust being developed by the engine. pas","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-013","question":"What is the electric source for a cylinder head temperature gauge?","idealAnswer":"A thermocouple attached to the cylinder oe head. ea","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-014","question":"Where is a carburetor air temperature bulb located?","idealAnswer":"It is in the air intake passage to the engine. bal =","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-015","question":"How can a turbine engine EGT be checked without operating the engine?","idealAnswer":"oa By checking resistance to thermocouples and circuits. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-H-016","question":"What is the typical acceptable resistance of a cylinder head thermocouple cable?","idealAnswer":"2 to 8 ohms. oe 78 ved e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-i","letter":"I","title":"I. Engine Fire Protection Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-I-001","question":"What are the main categories of engine fire protection systems?","idealAnswer":"Spot-detection, continuous-loop, and flame detectors,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-002","question":"When using a thermocouple type fire detector system, what happens if the engine overheats slowly?","idealAnswer":"A thermocouple depends on the rate of temperature rise, and will not give a warning if the engine slowly overheats or a short circuit develops.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-003","question":"How many thermocouples are required for engine fire detection?","idealAnswer":"The number of thermocouples used in an engine fire detection system depend on the size of the fire , zone and the total circuit resistance.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-004","question":"Where does a thermocouple fire detector system get its electrical power to operate?","idealAnswer":"The thermocouple produces power to close a relay, and power from the aircraft electrical system flows through the relay to the warning light.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-005","question":"Describe a thermal switch fire detector system?","idealAnswer":"Thermal switch systems have one or more lights that are energized by the aircraft's electrical - system when a heat sensitive thermal switch closes the circuit at a specific temperature.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-006","question":"How are thermal switches electrically connected in the circuit?","idealAnswer":"The thermal switches are connected in parallel with each other but in series with the warning light. A temperature rise in any one section of the circuit will cause a thermal switch to close and complete the circuit to indicate a fire or overheat condition.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-007","question":"What is the most common cause of a false fire warning in a continuous loop detector system?","idealAnswer":"The common causes of false warnings are dents, kinks, or crushed sensing elements. This damage causes the internal wire too short to the ground wire or outer tubing. 11. Will a continuous loop fire detector still indicate the presence of a fire if the element is broken in two? Yes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-008","question":"What two methods are used to discharge fire extinguishing agents?","idealAnswer":"Mechanical and electrical.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-009","question":"What method is used to release the fire extinguishing agent in a typical turbine engine fire protection system?","idealAnswer":"One common method is to equip the fire extinguishing containers with discharge valves that are operated by electrically discharged cartridges. The discharge plug is seated with a breakable disk that is ruptured by the explosive cartridge (squibs). The contents of the bottle are discharged into the engine area.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-010","question":"What method is used to determine proper fire extinguisher container pressure?","idealAnswer":"Check the container gauge to determine if the pressure is between the prescribed minimum and maximum limits.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-011","question":"What is the color marking used to identify fire extinguishing lines?","idealAnswer":"A brown tag with a row of black diamonds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-012","question":"What method other than the pressure gauge is used to indicate low agent pressure in a container?","idealAnswer":"Some aircraft are equipped with a low-pressure warning light in the cockpit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-013","question":"What is the purpose of the yellow and red discharge plugs (disks) in a turbine engine fire extinguishing system?","idealAnswer":"The yellow plug indicates a pilot-initiated discharge. The red plug (disk) indicates thermal activated discharge.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-014","question":"What are two methods commonly used to distribute the fire extinguishing agent to the engine?","idealAnswer":"A perforated tube or discharge nozzles are used to distribute the agent.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-015","question":"How does the fire extinguishing agent put out the fire?","idealAnswer":"It dilutes the atmosphere so that it will not support combustion.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-016","question":"What does \"HRD' stand for?","idealAnswer":"High rate of discharge.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-017","question":"How is the fire extinguishing agent distributed fram a HRD system?","idealAnswer":"It is delivered from open-end tubes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-018","question":"How long does it take to discharge the extinguishing agent in a HRD system?","idealAnswer":"It only takes 1 to 2 seconds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-I-019","question":"Which type of fire detection system gives a more complete coverage to a fire hazard?","idealAnswer":"A continuous-loop system. |","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-j","letter":"J","title":"J. Engine Electrical Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-J-001","question":"How would you seat newly installed generator brushes to the commutator?","idealAnswer":"When new brushes are installed in a generator the face of the brushes must be shaped to provide and area of maximum contact with the commutator. To seat the brushes, use fine grain sandpaper placed around the commutator with the sanding surface facing outward. Turn the armature ina normal direction of rotation until the face of the brushes is properly contoured. Remove the sandpaper and blow out any residue with compressed alr, 10 Steps 1. Remove Cover 2. Remove Brushes 3. Install Sandpaper 4. Install new brushes 5. Rotate Generator 6. Remove Brushes 7. Remove Sandpaper 8. Blow out Generator 9. Install New Brushes 10. install Cover","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-002","question":"When is it necessary to replace the brushes in a starter?","idealAnswer":"Once they reach half their original length.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-003","question":"Where is the generator rating and performance data located?","idealAnswer":"On the data plate attached to the generator.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-004","question":"What units make up a DC generator three-unit regulator?","idealAnswer":"The voltage regulator, current limiter, and reverse iP. gt 9) ig! current cutout.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-005","question":"What method is used to control the voltage of an aircraft alternator?","idealAnswer":"The voltage regulator.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-006","question":"What determines the frequency of the voltage of an alternator?","idealAnswer":"The frequency depends upon the speed of rotation of the rotor and the number of poles.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-007","question":"How is alternator frequency maintained?","idealAnswer":"By installing a constant speed drive (CSD) unit between the engine . and the alternator.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-008","question":"What are the three basic types of DC motors?","idealAnswer":"Series motors, shunt motors, and compound motors.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-009","question":"Name the parts of aDC motor?","idealAnswer":"The armature, brushes, commutator, and field frame (ABC-F).","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-010","question":"Why is a starter-generator system, used on many turbine engines?","idealAnswer":"» To save space and weight. The starter-generator operates as a starter motor during engine starting. ' After the engine has reached a self-sustaining speed, it switches operation to a generator for supplying AC electrical power for aircraft systems. * .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-011","question":"Why should stranded wire be used in powerplant applications?","idealAnswer":"It is less likely to fatigue and break from vibrations.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-012","question":"What is the American Wire Gauge (AWG) system of designating electrical wire size?","idealAnswer":"A gauge number is assigned to the wire according to its cross-sectional area. The smaller the gauge number the larger the wire.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-013","question":"What is open wiring?","idealAnswer":"(Not an open circuit) Any wire, wire group, or wire bundle not enclosed in conduit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-014","question":"What methods are used to control the voltage output of DC aircraft generator?","idealAnswer":"The vibratin e voltage requlator, three-unit requlator, and carbon-pile. The only practical means of regulating generator voltage is to control the strength of the magnetic field. Field strength is determined by the amount of current flowing through the field coils, and the current is controlled by : placing some form of variable or intermittent resistance in the external field circuit of the generator.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-015","question":"What aircraft electrical system normally doesn't have a fuse or circuit breaker?","idealAnswer":"Starter circuit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-016","question":"Why are protective covers placed on some electrical switches?","idealAnswer":"To prevent the switch from accidentally being actuated.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-017","question":"What are the causes of excessive arcing at the generator brushes?","idealAnswer":"Arcing can be caused by a commutator that is . | dirty, rough, or out of round. A common cause of arcing is worn or binding brushes, or when brush spring tension is 4 too low. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-018","question":"When installing single wires or wire bundles, how much slack is normally allowed between supports?","idealAnswer":"Slack between supports should normally not exceed one-half inch.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-J-019","question":"What precaution should be taken when running wires close to heating ducts or exhaust stacks?","idealAnswer":"The wires should be insulated with a high temperature resistant material. \\ 1 | n ! | 82 . i tudaalehsatin ee Bs.9 shes Litre nie) cua divanksoctnd aa Voediy a Gn AR eat SIs oe NT SEN ARR ARTIS hae a Tae ace Pn on ae ee aoa =~ oy","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-k","letter":"K","title":"K. Lubrication Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-K-001","question":"What is straight mineral oil?","idealAnswer":"It is a type of natural lubricating oil that has no additives for foaming, cleaning, or ~~ viscosity.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-002","question":"What is ashless dispersant (AD) oil?","idealAnswer":"It is a type of mineral oil with additives that evenly spread contaminates = throughout the oil. The additives that are used do not form ashes. \"9 3, whats synthetic oi? me Synthetic oil is an oil that is man-made through the process of chemically altering the Ey molecular structure of petroleum oil to achieve the desired characteristics. a. Whatismeant by multi-viscosity oil? ey It is a type of oil that has varying viscosity. When the oil is cold, it will have low viscosity, but Ey when it warms up, the viscosity will increase fur better lubrication. For example, 5w-30 has a =~ viscosity index of 5 when it is cold, but 30 when it is warm.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-003","question":"What are the purposes of reciprocating engine oil?","idealAnswer":"It cleans, cools, cushions, seals, and lubricates parts. 2 6. What Is the most Important property that aircraft reciprocating engine oll must possess? 2 Viscosity. (A measurement of frictional resistance to flow.) 2» 7. What factors must be considered in determining the proper grade of oil to use in a specific engine? » The operating load, rotational speeds, and operating temperatures are the most important factors 2» to be considered.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-004","question":"What are some oil contaminants?","idealAnswer":"Gasoline, moisture, acid, dirt, carbon, and metallic particles. 2 10. From what location on a reciprocating engine is the oil temperature usually taken? a» In either system, the bulb is located where it measures oil temperature before it enters the hot Pp) sections of the engine. In a dry sump lubrication system, the oil temperature bulb is located anywhere in the oil inlet line between the oil tank and the engine. Wet sump systems have the 2 temperature bulb located where it senses the temperature after the oil passes through the oil P cooler. 2 11. What do metallic particles on an oil screen indicate? Metallic particles may be an indication of internal failure of 4 the engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-005","question":"What could cause oil foaming?","idealAnswer":"Foaming can be caused by diluted oil, contaminated oil, or the oil level is too 2? high.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-006","question":"What would be an indication of blocked oil cooler passages?","idealAnswer":"High oil temperature. 3 14. What would be an indication of an inadequate oil supply? Low oil pressure and high oil temperature. 3 83 LY SE a ET PEN 8 eS en","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-007","question":"What type of oil is used in turbine engine lubrication systems?","idealAnswer":"Specially developed synthetic oils.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-008","question":"Where can you find the specified grade of oil for a particular aircraft?","idealAnswer":"Oil grade can be found in the Airplane Flight Manual (AFM), or the Pilot's Operating Handbook (POH).","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-009","question":"What are two types of oil coolers used in turbine engine lubrication systems?","idealAnswer":"The air-cooled oil cooler and fuel-cooled oil cooler are the two basic types in general use.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-010","question":"What is the meaning of oil flash point?","idealAnswer":"Fire point? 4 Oil flash point is the temperature at which the oil will begin to give off ignitable vapors. The fire 4 point is the temperature at which there are sufficient vapors to support a continuous flame.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-011","question":"What is the weight of aircraft engine oil?","idealAnswer":"Approximately 7.5 pounds per U.S. gallon. 4 ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-012","question":"What is the purpose of an oil dilution system?","idealAnswer":"The oil dilution system thins the oil by introducing fuel into the lubrication system which makes cold . weather starting easier. Fuel is used because it will evaporate out of the oil and leave through the ‘ : crankcase breather. j","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-013","question":"What is the primary purpose of engine oil lubricant?","idealAnswer":"To reduce friction. . ‘ 3 22. What is the S.0.A.P. analysis? $.0.A.P. is an oil analysis system that establishes a trend to measure engine j health. One test does not do much unless something is very wrong.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-014","question":"Where are carbon seals used in turbine engines?","idealAnswer":"Carbon seals are used in aircraft gearboxes, air-turbine motor , starters, and main shaft seals. i","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-015","question":"What is the key purpose of lubrication in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"For lubrication of the main shaft bearings. ‘ ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-016","question":"Where are engine oil filters located?","idealAnswer":"They are located between the oil pump output and the engine bearings. é","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-017","question":"Why do reciprocating engines require oil with a high viscosity index?","idealAnswer":"(Small changes in viscosity over a wide 2 temperature range) Higher viscosity indexes allow for easier starting in cold temperatures and prevents the oil fram 2 becoming too thin at operating temperatures to protect bearings under operating loads.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-018","question":"Why do reciprocating engines require high viscosity oil?","idealAnswer":"(oil flows slowly) Higher viscosity oil performs better in reciprocating engines because they have large clearances, high operating temperatures, and high bearing pressures.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-K-019","question":"What two factors impact fluid viscosity?","idealAnswer":"Temperature and pressure, although pressure has a very small effect on viscosity. -","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-l","letter":"L","title":"L. Ignition and Starting Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-L-001","question":"What is a magneto?","idealAnswer":"A magneto is a self-contained AC generator driven by the engine for the purpose of ignition in a reciprocating engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-002","question":"Why are magneto ignition systems preferred over battery type ignition systems for reciprocating engines?","idealAnswer":"Magnetos are an independent ignition source and do not rely on battery power to provide ignition.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-003","question":"What can cause a reciprocating engine to continue to run when the ignition switch is in the \"off' position?","idealAnswer":"If there is a break in the p-lead wire from the magneto to the switch, then the ignition switch cannot ground the magneto primary circuit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-004","question":"What is the purpose of the condenser in a high-tension magneto electrical system?","idealAnswer":"To prevent arcing at the points and to hasten the collapse of the magnetic field around the primary coil,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-005","question":"Where is the E-gap position?","idealAnswer":"The E-gap position is when the poles of the rotating magnet are a few degrees past the neutral Position. E-gap is the position at which the points begin to Open.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-006","question":"What are the three major circuits of a high-tension magneto system?","idealAnswer":"The magnetic circuit, primary electrical circuit, and secondary electrical circuit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-007","question":"What are the components of a high-tension magnetic circuit?","idealAnswer":"The magnetic circuit consists of a permanent rotating magnet, soft iron core, and pole shoes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-008","question":"What happens when the primary breaker points open in a magneto?","idealAnswer":"- Opening the breaker points stops the flow of current in the primary coil. The magnetic field that was emitted by the primary coil collapses. As this magnetic field collapses, the flux lines that cut across the windings of the secondary coil induce a pulse of high voltage current in the * secondary coil needed to fire the spark plugs.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-009","question":"What is the piston position when the spark occurs in a cylinder?","idealAnswer":"The piston is a specified number of degrees before top dead center on the compression stroke. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-010","question":"What is a dual magneto?","idealAnswer":"The dual magneto incorporates two magnetos in one housing. They share the rotating magnet a and cam, however they have their own sets of points, coils, and distributer blocks.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-011","question":"What is the difference between a low tension and a high-tension ignition system?","idealAnswer":"In the high-tension system, the high voltage is generated in the magneto and flows too the e plugs through high tension leads. In low tension systems, the low voltage is generated in the magneto and flows through low-tension leads to the primary winding of a transformer coil 2 located near each spark plug. There the voltage is increased to high voltage by transformer action and is conducted to the plugs by very short high-tension leads.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-012","question":"When the ignition switch is in the OFF position, what is the condition of the primary circuit?","idealAnswer":"It is completed through the ignition switch to ground.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-013","question":"What three conditions are required to fire plugs in a cylinder when the piston is in the prescribed position?","idealAnswer":"The ‘ magneto's magnet poles must be in the E-gap position, the breaker points must open, and the distributor rotor rl must be aligned with a distributor electrode for that cylinder.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-014","question":"What is a dual ignition system, and what part of the system is grounded when the ignition switch is placed in the RIGHT position?","idealAnswer":"A dual ignition system uses two separate magnetos with the harness arranged so that each ' magneto fires one plug in each cylinder. If the switch is set to right, the left magneto will be grounded, preventing it ; from firing.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-015","question":"What is internal timing of a magneto?","idealAnswer":"It is adjusting the breaker points to open when the poles of the rotating ' magnet are at the E-gap position.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-016","question":"What is the purpose of an impulse coupling used with a magneto?","idealAnswer":"The impulse coupling is used to retard the timing of the spark to after top dead center for starting the ‘ engine. It provides a momentarily high rotational speed to produce a spark at low crankshaft rpm.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-017","question":"What is staggered ignition timing?","idealAnswer":"Itis the firing of one spark plug before the other. Instead of firing them simultaneously.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-018","question":"What is the proper spark plug reach?","idealAnswer":"The proper spark plug reach is when the electrode end of the plug inside the cylinder is in the best position to achieve ignition.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-019","question":"Why is it important to keep track of the cylinders that spark plugs are installed in?","idealAnswer":"Because spark plugs can provide a lot of information about the health of a specific cylinder.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-020","question":"What type ignition system is used in most turbine engines?","idealAnswer":"Atypical turbine engine is equipped with an electronic capacitor type ignition system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-021","question":"What is the function of the igniter plugs in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"The function of the igniter plug is to provide a discharge gap for the current, which is stored in the capacitor. The discharge results in a high intensity spark which ignites the fuel-air mixture.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-022","question":"When are turbine engine ignition systems normally in use?","idealAnswer":"The ignition system is required only for starting or restarting an engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-023","question":"What are two types of igniter plugs?","idealAnswer":"Annular gap - A long-reach plug that extends into the combustion chamber. Constrained gap - A short-reach plug that does not extend into the chamber because the spark does not remain close to the plug.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-024","question":"What is the purpose of the dual magneto configuration?","idealAnswer":"For redundancy and safety, in case one magneto fails. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-025","question":"How can you tell if a starter commutator is dirty or worn?","idealAnswer":"An excessively noisy starter.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-026","question":"What does it mean when the starter relay clicks when the starter switch is placed in the \"start\" position?","idealAnswer":"This indicates that the supply voltage is too low to supply enough current to the relay coil to fully engage the contact points.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-027","question":"What is an advantage of air-turbine starters, when compared to electric starters, for large turbine engines?","idealAnswer":"They are light weight, and do not have the same cool down periods as electric starters.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-028","question":"Why do turbine ignitors not get carbon fouled as often as reciprocating engine spark plugs?","idealAnswer":"Due to extremely high rates of electrical discharge in the turbine ignitors.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-029","question":"What are the three parts of an ignition systems of a turbine?","idealAnswer":"Exciter, Ignition lead, Ignitor","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-L-030","question":"What is the difference between a temporary magnet and a permanent magnet?","idealAnswer":"A permanent magnet is one that retains its magnetic properties for a long period of time. Temporary magnets also called an electromagnet are those that simply act like permanent magnets when they are within a strong magnetic field or have current flowing near them. ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-m","letter":"M","title":"M. Fuel Metering Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-M-001","question":"Where are the fuel spray nozzles located in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"They are located either externally or internally on the combustion chamber in such a way that the fuel can be sprayed into the combustion area.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-002","question":"What engine variables are sensed by a hydro mechanical fuel control?","idealAnswer":"The fuel control senses power lever position, rpm, compressor inlet pressure temperature, burner pressure, or compressor discharge pressure, and EGT or TIT.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-003","question":"What are the two most commonly used carburetors in small reciprocating engines?","idealAnswer":"They are the float-type carburetor and pressure-type carburetor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-004","question":"What type of fuel control unit is used in a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"Both hydro mechanical and electronic fuel control units are in use today. However, the most common fuel control unit is the hydro mechanical type.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-005","question":"What is meant by trimming a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"Adjusting the fuel control for the proper specific gravity of the fuel and for the proper idle and full throttle RPM.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-006","question":"When should a turbine engine be trimmed?","idealAnswer":"Whenever there is a loss in engine power, or the manufacturer specifies.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-007","question":"What specifications are used to trim a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"The data plate on the engine typically contains the trimming specifications.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-008","question":"What are two types of electronic fuel controls for turbine engines?","idealAnswer":"Supervisory EEC (Electronic Engine Control) and FADEC (Full-Authority Digital Electronic Control). (There is also a 3rd type called mechanical)","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-009","question":"What is a FADEC?","idealAnswer":"A Full-Authority Digital Electronic Control. It is a high-precision digital electronic fuel control that utilizes an Electronic Engine Control and the Flight Management Computer to measure the fuel nozzles that prevent over-speed and over-temperature conditions. The FADEC also sends information to indication systems for the aircraft crew.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-010","question":"Does a turbine engine controlled by a FADEC require manual trimming?","idealAnswer":"No, the FADEC Is self-trimming.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-011","question":"What is the purpose of a mixture control in a float type carburetor?","idealAnswer":"It is to control the fuel-air mixture as air density changes with altitude changes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-012","question":"What is the purpose of an accelerating system on a float-type carburetor?","idealAnswer":"When the throttle is opened rapidly, the fuel-air mixture will lean out momentarily. To overcome this tendency, a charge of fuel from the accelerating pump will temporarily enrich the mixture in the Venturi.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-013","question":"Describe the purpose and operation of a venturi?","idealAnswer":"The venturi in a carburetor is an example of Bernoulli's Principle, which states \"As the velocity of a gas or liquid increases, the pressure decreases.\" The venturi performs three functions. It Proportions the fuel-air mixture, lowers the pressure at the discharge nozzle, and limits airflow at full throttle. The principal operation of the carburetor depends on the differential between the atmospheric pressure, and the decreased pressure offered by the venturi.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-014","question":"What is the function of a metering jet?","idealAnswer":"Its purpose is to limit the fuel flow when the throttle valve is wide open. The main metering jet is * placed in the fuel passage between the float chamber and the discharge nozzle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-015","question":"What is the fuel metering force in a float-type carburetor?","idealAnswer":"It is differential pressure between the pressure in the float chamber and at the nozzle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-016","question":"What will be the result of using excessively rich idle mixtures?","idealAnswer":"Excessively rich idle mixtures will cause carbon fouling of the spark plug. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-017","question":"Where does a pressure injection carburetor obtain fuel pressure?","idealAnswer":"It obtains fuel pressure from the boost pump for starting and from the engine driven fuel pump for normal operations.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-018","question":"What operates an automatic mixture control (AMC) on a pressure carburetor?","idealAnswer":"The AMC contains sealed bellows that expand or contract with changes in atmospheric pressure. . The movement of the bellow operates a tapered needle that controls the impact air pressure into the ' \"A\" chamber of the carburetor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-019","question":"In what position do you place the mixture control of a pressure injection carburetor to stop the engine?","idealAnswer":": In the idle cutoff position.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-020","question":"What rpm indication should result when the mixture control is placed in the idle cutoff position when the idle mixture is set correctly?","idealAnswer":"There should be a slight increase in rpm, followed by a rapid drop of rpm to zero.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-021","question":"What is the purpose of filling a pressure injection carburetor full of fuel and allowing it to soak for a period of about eight hours prior to installation?","idealAnswer":"To soften the diaphragms and make them as pliable as they were when the carburetor was originally calibrated.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-022","question":"What are three types of carburetor icing?","idealAnswer":"Throttle icing, impact icing, and fuel evaporation icing.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-M-023","question":"What is the typical fuel pressure in a float type carburetor?","idealAnswer":"3-5Spsi.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-n","letter":"N","title":"N. Engine Fuel Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-N-001","question":"What is the purpose of filters in a fuel system?","idealAnswer":"They are used to prevent foreign matter from entering the carburetor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-002","question":"Why do carburetors in aircraft have a mixture control?","idealAnswer":"Because air density changes with altitude, which affects . the air-fuel mixture. The mixture control provides a means to adjust for the correct mixture at different altitudes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-003","question":"What two items are adjusted when setting idling conditions on a float carburetor?","idealAnswer":"The throttle stop for idling RPM, and the idle mixture through the idle needle valve.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-004","question":"What is the purpose of fuel selector valves?","idealAnswer":"They provide a means of shutting off fuel flow, cross-feed for tank 1 and engine selection and for fuel transfer.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-005","question":"What should be looked for when inspecting an engine driven fuel pump?","idealAnswer":"Look for fuel leaks and security of ' mounting. '","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-006","question":"What is the purpose of an engine driven fuel pump?","idealAnswer":"The engine driven fuel pump always delivers a continuous ‘ supply of fuel at the proper pressure during engine operation. ' ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-007","question":"What type of fuel pump is generally used with large reciprocating engines?","idealAnswer":"A positive-displacement rotary-vane- type pump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-008","question":"What happens to the excess fuel not required by the engine in a constant displacement pump?","idealAnswer":"The pressure relief valve opens, and the fuel is routed back to the inlet side of the pump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-009","question":"What is the most common type of fuel boost pump?","idealAnswer":"The electrically driven centrifugal type pump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-010","question":"What are the functions of the boost pump?","idealAnswer":"First, during starting the boost pump supplies fuel to the engine pump for starting. Also, they are used to maintain pressure in the fuel lines to prevent the fuel from boiling off at altitude. Finally, i boost pumps act as a backup if the engine driven pump fails.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-011","question":"What is the purpose of the bypass valve in the engine driven fuel pump?","idealAnswer":"s The bypass valve provides a path around the pump vanes for starting the engine, and to allow fuel to bypass the pump in case of a pump failure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-012","question":"What is the purpose of using boost pumps in a fuel system?","idealAnswer":"Electric boost pumps are used to supply fuel to a pressure carburetor during engine starting and in case of engine driven pump failure. However, a main function of a boost pump to keep the pressure on the suction side of the engine driven pump from becoming low enough to permit the fuel to boil when operating at high altitudes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-013","question":"What are the three general causes of vapor lock?","idealAnswer":"Low fuel pressure, high fuel temperatures, and excessive fuel turbulence. 14. Turbine engine fuel pumps may be divided into what two distinct system categories? Constant displacement and variable displacement. oe Cea a ae re","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-014","question":"What category is a turbine engine driven gear-type pump?","idealAnswer":"Constant displacement.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-015","question":"What type of fuel pump can be made to vary the fuel flow at any speed?","idealAnswer":"A variable displacement pump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-016","question":"What part of a gas turbine engine fuel system is very susceptible to the formation of ice?","idealAnswer":"The fuel filters.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-017","question":"How is the engine fuel system protected from ice formation?","idealAnswer":"Using fuel heaters.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-018","question":"Why is a micron fuel filter provided with a bypass valve as a necessary safety factor?","idealAnswer":"Because the small openings in this type of filter make it very susceptible to clogging.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-019","question":"Where are fuel filters located?","idealAnswer":"They are located at the fuel pump, in line with the system; \"Last chance\" filter screens are in the fuel controls.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-N-020","question":"Can you use automotive gasoline in a reciprocating engine in place of Avgas?","idealAnswer":"Yes, if you have a STC.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-o","letter":"O","title":"O. Induction and Engine Airflow Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-O-001","question":"What are the three major parts of a reciprocating engine induction system?","idealAnswer":"} The air scoop, fuel delivery system, and intake manifold.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-002","question":"What effect does induction system icing have on engine performance?","idealAnswer":"Induction system icing can cause an engine to act erratically and lose power and may possibly quit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-003","question":"What method is used to prevent or remove induction system ice in a reciprocating engine?","idealAnswer":"} The most common method of preventing or removing ice formation in the induction system is by the t use of heated air.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-004","question":"How is ice cleared in some aircraft induction systems if the carburetor heat is too low to clear the ice?","idealAnswer":"Anti-icing fluids such as alcohol, is sprayed into the induction system ahead of the carburetor. The fluid will dislodge the Ice which Is Ingested by the engine. ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-005","question":"What engine indication can he the result of a dirty air filter?","idealAnswer":"Low power.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-006","question":"What is the danger of using carburetor heat when operating at high power settings?","idealAnswer":"The higher air temperatures could cause detonation and possible engine failure. | 7. Carburetor throttle ice is most likely to form when the throttle is in which position? t When the throttle butterfly is in the partially closed position.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-007","question":"What are two general classifications of superchargers used in reciprocating engine induction systems?","idealAnswer":"They are classified as internally driven, or externally driven supercharger systems.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-008","question":"What is the purpose of a distribution impeller in a radial engine?","idealAnswer":"It is designed to break up the fuel globules into finer particles for better distribution to the cylinders.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-009","question":"How does an internally driven supercharger get its power?","idealAnswer":"Internally driven superchargers are mechanically engine driven. A high-speed impeller is driven through a gear train from the crankshaft. The use of gear ratios can be single stage or two speed. A two speed gear ratio normally varies from 6:1 to 12:1.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-010","question":"Where does an externally driven supercharger get its power?","idealAnswer":"i By the exhaust gases that are directed against a turbine. For this reason, they are commonly called } turbochargers.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-011","question":"What unit regulates the amount of exhaust gases to the turbine of a turbocharger?","idealAnswer":"The waste gate.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-012","question":"What type of power is used to control the position of the turbocharger waste gate on some engines?","idealAnswer":"Oil pressure to an actuating piston.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-O-013","question":"What can cause the waste gate to stick?","idealAnswer":"A buildup of carbon can cause the waste gate to stick","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-p","letter":"P","title":"P. Engine Cooling Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-P-001","question":"What is the most common means of regulating the cooling air flow through a reciprocating engine?","idealAnswer":"Using cowl flaps.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-002","question":"What is the purpose of the fins on engine cylinders?","idealAnswer":"To increase the surface area of the cylinder for cooling.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-003","question":"What other engine characteristics are designed to aid in engine cooling besides cooling fins?","idealAnswer":"The engine cowling and baffles are designed to force air through the cylinder cooling fins.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-004","question":"Why is the \"open and clase\" adjustment during installation of cowl flaps important?","idealAnswer":"For each engine installation, the cowl flaps are set for a tolerance that will permit them to open and close in the correct amount to keep the cylinder head temperature within allowable limits. 5S. What should be done to cylinders when too much of the cooling fin area is broken off? The cylinder should be replaced because it cannot cool properly, and a hotspot will develop.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-005","question":"When should cowl flaps be kept in the fully open position?","idealAnswer":"Normally during all ground run operations.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-006","question":"What publication should be referred to before re-profiling cylinder cooling fins?","idealAnswer":"The manufacturer's service, or overhaul manual should be used to obtain the allowable limits.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-007","question":"What power sources are used to operate the cowl flaps?","idealAnswer":"Cowl flaps may be operated by electrical power, hydraulic power, or manually.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-008","question":"What is the purpose of blast tubes?","idealAnswer":". To direct jets of cooling air to accessories of the engine that have high heat concentrations (such as a generator.) On radial engines they duct air onto the rear spark plug elbows of each cylinder to prevent overheating of the ignition leads.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-009","question":"What are the main reasons that excessive heat in a reciprocating engine is undesirable?","idealAnswer":". Excessive heat shortens the life of the engine parts, impairs lubrication, and affects combustion.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-010","question":"What is the source of air directed to engine bearings for cooling?","idealAnswer":"Bleed air from the compressor section of the engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-011","question":"What is the purpose of insulation on the exhaust duct of a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"a To reduce the temperature of the structures in the vicinity of the exhaust duct or afterburner, and to eliminate the possibility of fuel or oil coming into contact with the hot parts of the engine. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-012","question":"What types of materials are used to make insulation blankets for turbine engines?","idealAnswer":", They are made of stainless steel, layers of aluminum foil, fiber glass, and silver foil. cmammrser amas sine eetetaeeereeeneee el = RT","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-013","question":"What areas of a turbine engine are cooled by the secondary air passing through the engine?","idealAnswer":"The combustion chamber and the turbines.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-014","question":"What is the purpose of an engine cowling?","idealAnswer":"It streamlines the engine to reduce drag and forms an envelope : around the engine, which forces the airstream between the cylinders to cool them.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-P-015","question":"What is the purpose of an oil cooler bypass valve?","idealAnswer":"It allows the thick oil to bypass the cooler when the oil is cold. G ' t t ft ¢ t t c « t € e ¢ t ' t Y FETT EE MRR EY SSP EONS RCE te = : :","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-q","letter":"Q","title":"Q. Engine Exhaust and Reverser Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-001","question":"What are two types of reciprocating engine exhaust systems?","idealAnswer":"The short stack system and collector system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-002","question":"What are the possible hazards of exhaust system failure?","idealAnswer":"It can result in carbon monoxide poisoning of the crew and passengers, or partial or complete loss of engine power and an aircraft fire.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-003","question":"What type of exhaust system is generally used on low powered non supercharged engines?","idealAnswer":"The short stack system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-004","question":"What type of exhaust system is used on turbocharged engines?","idealAnswer":"The exhaust collector system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-005","question":"What type of exhaust system creates a higher exhaust back pressure?","idealAnswer":"The collector system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-006","question":"What is the purpose of a reciprocating engine exhaust system?","idealAnswer":"It is used to remove heat and noxious gases from the engine nacelle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-007","question":"What happens when lead, zinc, or galvanized marks are made onan exhaust system?","idealAnswer":"The mark is absorbed by the metal when heated and causes a change in the molecular structure that weakens the metal.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-008","question":"What type of visual indication can usually be seen in the area of an exhaust leak?","idealAnswer":"Exhaust leaks usually leave flat gray or sooty black carbon streaks on the pipes in the area of the leak.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-009","question":"How are ceramic coated exhaust stacks cleaned?","idealAnswer":"By the degreasing method only.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-010","question":"What is the usual cause of muffler and heat exchanger failures?","idealAnswer":"They are usually caused by concentrated areas of thermal stress, or vibration stress.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-011","question":"What is the most common form of damage in exhaust systems?","idealAnswer":", Cracks that are the result of repeated expansion and contraction.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-012","question":"What should be the position of the waste gate when starting an engine?","idealAnswer":"Fully open. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-013","question":"Where are exhaust manifold and stack failures most likely to occur?","idealAnswer":"e These failures usually occur at welds, flanges, or clamped points in the system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-014","question":"What is a common cause of the waste gate unit malfunctioning in a turbocharger system?","idealAnswer":". The most common cause of waste gate malfunctioning is carbon buildup, \"coke buildup\" causing e the waste gate valve to stick in the closed position.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-015","question":"What factors are affected if the area of the exhaust nozzle of a turbine engine is changed?","idealAnswer":"e The size of the exhaust nozzle affects both the engine performance and the exhaust gas e temperature.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-016","question":"Why are thrust reversers not normally used during ground operations?","idealAnswer":". The danger of blowing foreign objects up into the intake stream is too great. -","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-017","question":"What are the probes in a turbine exhaust pipe used for?","idealAnswer":"They are used to measure exhaust gas temperature (EGT) and pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-018","question":"Why do you disassemble and inspect the collector type exhaust?","idealAnswer":"Exhaust system failure can result in carbon monoxide poisoning of crew and passengers.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-Q-019","question":"What is the purpose of the waste gate on a turbo-charged exhaust?","idealAnswer":"This valve controls the amount of exhaust gases forced through the turbo charger turbine. When the gate is fully closed all gases must pass through the turbine","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-r","letter":"R","title":"R. Propellers","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-R-001","question":"What is the purpose of a propeller?","idealAnswer":"To create thrust and either pull or push the airplane through the air.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-002","question":"What are the two types of propeller configurations?","idealAnswer":"The tractor type and pusher type.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-003","question":"What type of propeller has the blade angle built into the propeller and cannot be changed?","idealAnswer":"A fixed pitch propeller.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-004","question":"What is the purpose of the metal tipping on a leading edge of a wooden propeller?","idealAnswer":"It is to protect the propeller from damage caused by FOD (foreign object damage) in the air, during . landing, taxiing, and takeoff.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-005","question":"What tool is used to determine propeller blade angle?","idealAnswer":"A universal propeller protractor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-006","question":"What are the aerodynamic forces and loads acting on a rotating propeller blade?","idealAnswer":"~ A rotating propeller is acted upon by centrifugal force, aerodynamic twisting, centrifugal twisting, torque bending and thrust bending forces.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-007","question":"What is the meaning of propeller blade \"back\" and \"face?","idealAnswer":"\" The curved side of the propeller is called the blade back. This is the side that faces away from the engine. The flat side of the propeller is known as the blade face. This side of the blade faces the engine. If you are sitting in the cockpit, you will be looking at the face of the blade. -","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-008","question":"What should be used to clean aluminum and steel propeller blades and hubs?","idealAnswer":": They should be washed with a suitable cleaning solvent using a brush or cloth. a 2 9. What positions are used on a balance stand to check a two bladed propeller for static balance? m4 First the vertical position, and then the horizontal position. L ) 10. Cana fixed pitch propeller's pitch be changed? No, they are fixed at a specific pitch angle. ¢","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-009","question":"Why do you put the blades of a counterweight propeller into high pitch before stopping the engine?","idealAnswer":"bd This type of propeller has a movable cylinder that slides over a fixed piston. In the low pitch r) position, the cylinder is outboard, and the piston is exposed to the open air. In the high pitch a position, they cylinder moves inboard and covers the piston. In this position, the piston is protected from dirt and moisture in the air. This is particularly important if the airplane engine will not be s operated for several days. e@","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-010","question":"Why are cones installed on splined shafts with a propeller?","idealAnswer":"The cones center the propeller on the shaft as they ° are forced toward each other by the tightening of the retaining nut.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-011","question":"How is feathering accomplished on a constant speed counterweight propeller?","idealAnswer":"ry By releasing governor oil pressure on the counterweights. The feathering spring moves the blades e to the feathered position and the oil returns to the engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-012","question":"What types of systems are used for deicing propellers?","idealAnswer":"Fluid or electrical deicing systems. s e e iE ll,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-013","question":"What is the purpose of a slinger ring on some propeller installations?","idealAnswer":"Propeller deicing fluid is ejected from a stationary nozzle on the engine into a U-shaped channel i attached to the rear of the propeller assembly. The U-shaped channel is called the slinger ring. The i fluid under pressure of centrifugal force is transferred through small tubes to the propeller's leading | edge.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-014","question":"What is blade tracking?","idealAnswer":"It is the process of determining the position of the tips of the propeller blades relative to each other.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-015","question":"Do the counterweights on a constant speed propeller increase or decrease the blade angle?","idealAnswer":"The centrifugal forces of the counterweights increase the pitch of the blades.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-016","question":"What should you adjust if a pilot request 50 more rpm on an aircraft equipped with a constant speed propeller?","idealAnswer":"Adjust the governor's prop-lever set screw.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-017","question":"What adjustments are powerplant mechanics allowed to make to the prop governor?","idealAnswer":"Only the maximum RPM stop.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-018","question":"Why are propellers indexed to the crankshaft of an engine?","idealAnswer":"To reduce vibrations to a minimum.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-019","question":"Can transverse cracks in a propeller be repaired?","idealAnswer":"No, transverse cracks of any size are unacceptable.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-020","question":"What is the alpha range and beta range of a propeller?","idealAnswer":"Beta range is the condition of the propeller during ground operations and in the reverse pitch position. Alpha range is the propeller position when the aircraft is in flight.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-021","question":"When changing metal bushings on a wooden propeller assembly, a thorough inspection of the bolt holes should be accomplished. Why?","idealAnswer":"To check for elongation of the holes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-022","question":"What happens to a constant speed feathering propeller when the propeller governor's oil pressure drops to 0 psi?","idealAnswer":"The low pitch stop engages to prevent blade angle changes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-R-023","question":"What is the purpose of an arbor when balancing a propeller?","idealAnswer":"To support the propeller on the balance knives. :","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"powerplant","key":"baker-powerplant-s","letter":"S","title":"S. Turbine Powered Auxiliary Power Units","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-P-S-001","question":"Where does the APU fuel supply normally come from?","idealAnswer":"The airplane's main fuel supply.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-002","question":"How is the APU rotated dunng start up?","idealAnswer":"With an electric starter. 4+. What is the function of an APU air inlet plenum? It is to stabilize the pressure of the air before it enters the « compressor. '","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-003","question":"When operating at high loads. what is the speed of an APU?","idealAnswer":"The APU remains at or near rated speed regardless of the load condition. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-004","question":"What is the effect of the pneumatic power when being used in conjunction with maximum APU shaft power . output?","idealAnswer":"The pneumatic loading will be automatically modulated to maintain a safe EGT.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-005","question":"Before shutting down, how would APU cooling be accomplished?","idealAnswer":"By closing the bleed air valve, thus removing e the load.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-006","question":"When is most of the load placed on an APU?","idealAnswer":"When the bleed air is opened. y","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-007","question":"What regulates fuel scheduling during APU start and under varying pneumatic bleed and electrical loads?","idealAnswer":"s Fuel scheduling is maintained automatically by the APU fuel control system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-008","question":"How is most troubleshooting accomplished on modern APUs?","idealAnswer":"By gathering information from the FADEC. »","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-009","question":"What is the primary function of the load compressor on APUs equipped with a free turbine and load ® compressor?","idealAnswer":"To provide bleed air for aircraft pneumatic systems. ‘i 12. To prevent rotor seizure \"shock cooling,\" how long should an APU idle before being shut down? 3 to 5 minutes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-P-S-010","question":"What is the function of the APU on a modern transport category aircraft?","idealAnswer":"Provides Bleed air for engine starting, air conditioning, de-icing. i s","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]}];
